{"step_index":49,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T16:06:17Z","input_tokens":67753,"cache_read_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1636,"thinking":"While the installation task completes, the focus shifts to creating the test file. A review of the required tests is underway to guide its development. The core tests will cover registration/verification, access controls for listing and ordering, the complete happy path, and boundary conditions such as invalid order amounts and state transitions.\n\n\nThe test file preparation begins by outlining the necessary imports and helper functions, including those for assertions, contract interactions, and time manipulation. A comprehensive `beforeEach` fixture will set up the environment, deploying contracts, assigning roles, registering users, and granting token allowances to accurately simulate marketplace interactions. Individual tests will then be designed to validate each functionality, like buyer cancellation, auto-release after shipment, dispute resolution, and supply chain tracking.\n\n\nVerification checks are implemented correctly for buyer and transporter roles. Listing a batch requires farmer verification, as demonstrated by the error when an unverified user or buyer attempts this action, while a farmer's listing succeeds.\n\nThe happy path tests the complete order lifecycle: listing, ordering, shipping, delivery, and payment with correct fee calculations, including escrow holding and release. Unverified buyers cannot create orders, and attempting to order more than the available quantity fails.\n\nState transitions are enforced, preventing actions like delivery before shipping. Buyer cancellation scenarios also need to be tested.\n\n\nTests for buyer cancellation before shipment will verify refunds and quantity restoration. After shipment, cancellation attempts must revert, confirming state transition enforcement.\n\nAutomatic release after a defined period needs time manipulation to trigger payment and fee distribution, testing for accurate calculations. Dispute resolution tests involve simulating arbiter actions, verifying balance updates for both parties according to the split.\n\nFinally, supply chain tracking tests will focus on checkpoint logging and retrieval, ensuring the history is accessible to the correct actors.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"status","TaskId":"d92e895e-1e4e-4b37-806a-2a55b4b85064/task-47"}}]}
